<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eleanor of Aquitaine</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1122–<a href="/years/1204">1204</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eleanor of Aquitaine was Duchess of Aquitaine and Countess of Poitou in her own right from 1137. She was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as wife of Louis VII, and after the annulment of that marriage she married Henry of Anjou in 1152, bringing Aquitaine into the Angevin Empire. She was the mother of the English kings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richard-i-of-england">Richard I</a> and John.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eleanor-of-aquitaine.jpg" alt="Eleanor of Aquitaine" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Adam Bishop · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Church_of_Fontevraud_Abbey_Eleanor_of_Aquitaine_effigy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke William IX (1071–1126) is the earliest troubadour whose songs survive, eleven poems in Occitan that range from the bawdy to the courtly, and the courts of his son William X and his granddaughter <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> were associated with poets such as Marcabru and Cercamon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Duchy of Aquitaine · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Some women ruled in their own right: <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> became duchess of Aquitaine at about fifteen and was queen of France and then of England.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li><li><blockquote>Archbishop Geoffrey of Bordeaux oversaw the wedding of <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> and Prince Louis in the Cathedral of Saint-André in Bordeaux.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-25">25 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry of Anjou, the future Henry II of England, married <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> after the annulment of her marriage to King Louis VII of France.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-18">18 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Henry II inherited Anjou, Maine and Touraine from his father Geoffrey Plantagenet and Normandy through his mother Matilda, gained Aquitaine by marrying <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> in 1152, and became king of England in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/state/angevin-empire">Angevin Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Duke William IX, the first known troubadour, wrote poetry in Occitan, and his granddaughter <b>Eleanor of Aquitaine</b> carried the county first to Louis VII of the Kingdom of France and then to Henry II of England.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-poitou">County of Poitou</a></li></ul></section>
